<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christoph Martin Wieland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1733">1733</a>–<a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christoph Martin Wieland was a German poet and novelist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, born near Biberach, where he served as town clerk from 1760 to 1769. He translated twenty-two of Shakespeare's plays into German and wrote the novel Geschichte des Agathon and the verse epic Oberon. In 1772 Duchess Anna Amalia called him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> as tutor to her sons, the start of the city's classical age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christoph-martin-wieland.jpg" alt="Christoph Martin Wieland" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ferdinand Jagemann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weimar_Anna_Amalia_Bibliothek@C.M._Wieland.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wieland was born on 5 September 1733 at Oberholzheim, half of which belonged to the Free Imperial City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biberach-an-der-riss">Biberach an der Riss</a> and half to Gutenzell Abbey. His father was a pastor, first at Oberholzheim and then at Biberach, and took great pains with his son's education. At twelve Wieland went to the Kloster Berge gymnasium near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, and by 1749 he was widely read in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> classics and the leading French writers. In 1750 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tubingen">University of Tübingen</a> to study law, but gave most of his time to literature.</p><p>The Swiss reformer J. J. Bodmer invited him to Zürich in 1752, but the two soon parted. Wieland stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> until 1760, spending the last year as a private tutor in Bern. He then returned to Biberach as director of the chancery, where the library of Count Stadion at Warthausen gave him access to French and English literature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He married Anna Dorothea von Hillenbrand on 21 October 1765, and they had 14 children. Between 1769 and 1772 he was professor of philosophy at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>. His pedagogic work Der goldene Spiegel (1772) drew the attention of Duchess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-amalia-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Anna Amalia of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</a>, who appointed him tutor to her sons Karl August and Constantin at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, which remained his home until his death.</p><p>In 1773 he founded Der teutsche Merkur, which under his editorship until 1789 became the most influential literary review in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Goethe attacked him in the satire Götter, Helden und Wieland, and Wieland answered with good nature. Goethe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a> were soon drawn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, where Anna Amalia formed a circle later joined by Schiller. Wieland died in Weimar on 20 January 1813.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Geschichte des Agathon (1766 to 1767) is regarded as the first Bildungsroman. Under the guise of a Greek fiction it describes his own spiritual and intellectual growth. He translated twenty-two of Shakespeare's plays into prose (1762 to 1766), the first attempt to present Shakespeare to the German public in something close to full. His light verse romances, such as Musarion (1768) and Der neue Amadis (1771), served as an antidote to the sentimental excesses of Sturm und Drang.</p><p>Die Abderiten (1774) satirises German provinciality, and his poetic masterpiece, the romantic epic Oberon (1780), became the basis of operas by Friederike Sophie Seyler and Carl Maria von Weber. Politically he was a moderate liberal who advocated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and a free press, and his thought was cosmopolitan: only a true cosmopolitan, he remarked, can be a good citizen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christoph Martin Wieland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duchess Anna Amalia, regent from 1758, invited <b>Christoph Martin Wieland</b> as tutor; her son Charles Augustus brought Goethe to Weimar in 1775, later followed by Herder and Schiller, while the University of Jena flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Christoph Martin Wieland</b>, born nearby, served as its town clerk from 1760 to 1769.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-biberach">Free Imperial City of Biberach</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oberholzheim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.26,9.92&amp;year=1785" aria-label="Oberholzheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4781 476 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christoph Martin Wieland"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5231 746h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5231 746h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.26,9.92&amp;year=1785">What was here in 1785? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
